      "id": "ch3-sec2",
      "num": "02",
      "title": "2. Three states of the market and the Break of Structure",
      "summary": "The host's first answer to \"what is market structure?\" was: uptrend is bullish, downtrend is bearish.",
      "figureId": "fig-ch3-2",
      "content": "The host's first answer to \"what is market structure?\" was: uptrend is bullish, downtrend is bearish. Roman agreed and added the third state: sometimes the market is in a range.\n\n### The Break of Structure (BOS)\n\nThe BOS is how structure is confirmed.\n\nBullish: every time price goes beyond the most recent swing high, it creates a Break of Structure. It is breaking out beyond the previous structure and going up, and that confirms the uptrend.\n\nBearish: every time price breaks below the most recent swing low, that confirms the downtrend.\n\nRoman: \"It is that easy, but also you need to practice.\" The host agreed that it sounds easy, and that the details come later.\n\n### The trading range\n\nOn a trending chart, the part of the move between the last confirmed swing high and swing low is called the current trading range (it was introduced last lesson). Roman draws his Fibonacci tool inside this range to get the premium and discount zones (Section 7). For a bearish example he pointed out a BOS beyond a lower low, then another BOS beyond the next lower low, and the leg after that is the current trading range.\n\n### Why a BOS matters\n\nA BOS tells you which side the market is on. While BOSs keep printing in one direction, you only look for trades in that direction. The moment the market stops printing them, or prints one the other way, you must stop and re-read the chart. That is exactly what the next sections are about.\n\n### Break of structure in a drawing\n\n> **Figure 2. Dashed lines show where each Break of Structure happens: price closes beyond the previous swing.**",
