      "id": "ch3-sec5",
      "num": "05",
      "title": "5. The fake Change of Character",
      "summary": "A fake Change of Character is a CHoCH that never got confirmed: price broke below a swing low, you assumed a bearish trend, and then the market kept going up.",
      "figureId": "fig-ch3-5",
      "content": "A fake Change of Character is a CHoCH that never got confirmed: price broke below a swing low, you assumed a bearish trend, and then the market kept going up.\n\n### You only know afterwards\n\nRoman is honest about the limit of this idea: you can only call it fake once price continues up and makes a new swing high. While you are trading live, you cannot say it is fake. Until then it remains a CHoCH, waiting to be either confirmed or proven fake. So as a prediction tool it is not very useful.\n\n### So why have the term at all?\n\n### Because of what it lets you do on the chart in hindsight and in the middle of the move. Roman's reasoning\n\nWithout the term, when price breaks down and then up again, you would call the second move \"another change of character\". Then you would have to say \"wait, this is not confirmed yet, I need price to break up again\" before you continue trading bullishly. You could not trade in that area.\n\nWith the term, you mark the first break as fake. Then the break up is just a normal Break of Structure, and you can take the trade.\n\nIn other words, marking it fake buys you time. You can open positions in that area, if you find good points of interest there, and you do not need to wait for any further confirmation, because the fake CHoCH has already told you the old trend is still alive. Roman: \"This is the only useful thing about that.\"\n\n### Quick summary\n\n### The fake Change of Character in a drawing\n\n> **Figure 5. Price breaks the low, then makes a new high. The CHoCH was fake, so the move up is a normal BOS.**",
