      "num": "06",
      "title": "6. Liquidity sweep or real break? The wick versus the body",
      "summary": "Roman showed a case you will see \"a lot of times on the chart\": price breaks down below the swing low, but only with a wick, not a candle body close.",
      "figureId": "fig-ch3-6",
      "content": "Roman showed a case you will see \"a lot of times on the chart\": price breaks down below the swing low, but only with a wick, not a candle body close.\n\nThat is a liquidity sweep.\n\nIt is not a Change of Character.\n\nIt is not a fake Change of Character either.\n\nPrice then continues up, \"business as usual\".\n\n### His words: \"This will be a sweep and that's it.\"\n\n### The comparison\n\n### Tricky cases are normal\n\nOn the SUI chart (Section 9) Roman said a break \"was actually a liquidity sweep, but it's tricky, you can say that this is also a change of character, because this is a confirmed swing low\". The lesson is not that the line between them is always sharp. It is that you decide with the body close and you stay flexible, because later candles will tell you more.\n\n### Why a sweep can be good for you\n\nIn one example price swept below a level and the stop loss of anyone sitting under it would have been hit. Roman's comment: there would not have been an entry there anyway. A sweep is a reason to be careful, not a signal to buy blindly.\n\n### Sweep, BOS and CHoCH in a drawing\n\n> **Figure 6. The body decides. A wick through the line is a sweep, a body close is a BOS (with the trend) or a CHoCH (against it).**",
