      "id": "ch3-sec8",
      "num": "08",
      "title": "8. Reading a big chart: slow markets make huge structures",
      "summary": "Bearish structure after a Change of Character.",
      "figureId": "fig-ch3-8",
      "content": "### After the theory, Roman put a long real chart on screen and read it from left to right. The story of the chart\n\nBearish structure after a Change of Character. Price went down.\n\nA break of structure confirmed the trend, then another.\n\nPrice then got stuck in a range for about two months.\n\nA break of structure below confirmed the bearish structure again, then another, then another.\n\nSuddenly a Change of Character appeared and price started going up.\n\nRoman prefers to mark such a range as a box, \"like this\", rather than marking every swing in it.\n\n### Two things to notice\n\nWhen the market slows down, the structures become huge. They take a lot of time to form. The host asked if he meant that the structure is very wide and takes a long time. Roman said yes.\n\nWhen the market starts moving, you see many more changes in structure. The host had noticed this over the weekend on his Bitcoin 4 hour chart: for months there was a high and a low and \"nothing in between\". Roman had told him he picked a boring one. Once price broke out of that big structure, small structures appeared again.\n\n### What this means for you\n\nA chart with few, wide structures is a slow, sleepy market. Be patient.\n\nA chart with many small structures is a fast market. More signals, but also more noise.\n\nThe size of the structure tells you the speed of the market, not the quality of the trade.\n\n### Big and small structures in a drawing\n\n> **Figure 8. A slow market draws few wide structures; a fast market draws many small ones.**",
