      "summary": "On the 1 hour chart he found a fair value gap, a possible point of interest.",
      "figureId": "fig-ch3-11",
      "content": "### Example A: a zone is not an entry (the no-trade case)\n\n### Price dropped into the zone, took a liquidity sweep, and would have stopped out anyone who had placed an order blindly. Roman then checked properly\n\nOn the 1 hour chart he found a fair value gap, a possible point of interest.\n\nHe went to a lower time frame (5 minutes was not available, so he used 10 minutes) to look for the entry pattern he had shown earlier.\n\nThe pattern did not appear inside the area, so there was no trade.\n\nThe pattern itself \"will come later\", he said. The lesson is: the optimal trade entry tool does not mean you enter there. You still need tools that identify good entries.\n\n### Example B: bearish structure, a trade that is \"not perfect\"\n\n### After a break of structure that confirmed the bearish trend, price re-entered the 0.5 area but did not quite reach the optimal trade entry. Roman and the host discussed\n\nA small untouched level there could be internal liquidity (to be explained in a later lesson).\n\nOn the 1 hour chart there was probably an order block, and liquidity from a consolidation of a few hours.\n\nBy the book the shift was late, so \"this is not perfect\". Roman checked that it was the correct range and called it a bad trade.\n\nBut a fair value gap gave a better entry, and that made it almost a 3 to 1 risk to reward: \"much better\".\n\n### Example C: a bullish reversal\n\nPrice broke up: CHoCH, then a BOS confirming the trend. Roman noticed a divergence on the RSI just before the CHoCH. He then drew the premium and discount zone, marked his points of interest (a couple of fair value gaps), and waited. Price completely took out the first gap, and then came the perfect entry.\n\n### Choosing your entry: three options\n\n### Roman described several valid ways to enter the same setup\n\n### The risk to reward discipline\n\nIt is a better decision to miss a trade than to get into a losing one with higher risk.\n\nIf the difference is between a 1.5 and a 2.5 risk to reward, do not take the 1.5: wait for the 2.5.\n\nMany traders Roman talks to never take a trade below 2.5. Anything above is fine. They place the order, close the laptop, and enjoy the day, because they know it is statistically a winning position and only news or something similar can ruin it.\n\n### Entry choices in a drawing\n\n> **Figure 11. A better entry price gives more reward for the same risk. Skip trades below about 2.5 risk to reward. The numbers are only illustrative.**",
