      "figureId": null,
      "content": "What the lesson description promised, and where it is in this chapter\n\n### Short notes on the two topics the transcript skipped\n\nThese are general Smart Money definitions added for completeness. They are not from the video, so check how the teachers define them in later lessons.\n\nImpulse move: a strong, fast move in the direction of the trend, the kind of move that usually breaks structure. Corrective move: the slower pull-back against it, which usually creates the swing you will later watch.\n\nStrong versus weak swing: a swing is usually called strong when it was responsible for breaking structure, and weak when it did not break anything and is likely to be taken out. In this video the closest idea is a confirmed swing low, one that has been followed by a confirming break.\n\n### Homework\n\nPractice round. The host said he needs to practise identifying breaks of structure so he does not end up alone at his chart wondering \"what did Roman tell me?\". Do the same: take a chart, mark swing highs and lows, then label each BOS and CHoCH.\n\nMark the range. On a sideways chart, draw a box and write \"no structure\".\n\nFake or real? For every CHoCH you find, write down whether it was confirmed (a second break) or fake (a new extreme the other way).\n\nDraw a Fibonacci zone on one trending chart and check whether price reacted at 0.5 or the optimal trade entry.\n\nTry the puzzles if you have access, and start the journal.\n\nSet one bar-close alert and compare it with a normal price alert.\n\n### Next lesson: liquidity\n\nThe next lesson is on liquidity, which Roman calls one of the most important topics after structure. His reason: \"liquidity is where the money is\", and \"liquidity is where prices reverse\". If you know exactly where the liquidity is, you can see the reversal \"coming from a mile away\". A new quiz on liquidity and other topics is planned too. Another live stream was announced for Thursday with a guest trader (the name is unclear in the transcript).",
